1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Querying inside documents. Dot paths, arrays, and when $elemMatch matters

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:12,400
Ticket 1001 embeds two events: a resident created it, an agent was assigned.

3
00:00:12,900 --> 00:00:18,100
A dotted path, in quotes, looks inside the nested requester document.

4
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:24,000
A plain value on an array field matches when the array contains it.

5
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:27,000
A tempting filter: two dotted conditions on the events array.

6
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:29,800
Condition 1, type "created": satisfied by event 5001.

7
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:32,600
Condition 2, actor_role "agent": satisfied by event 5002.

8
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:34,900
Each condition holds somewhere in the array, so the document matches.

9
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:39,900
But no single event says an agent created it. Valid query, wrong question.

10
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:47,200
$elemMatch tests each element against BOTH conditions together.

11
00:00:47,700 --> 00:00:52,900
No event is both 'created' and by an agent. The empty result is the right answer.

12
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:56,900
Event 5002 satisfies both conditions, so ticket 1001 qualifies.

13
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:00,400
It comes back with both stored events. Shaping the array is a separate projection job.

14
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Remember: "requester.user_id" reaches into a nested document. { tags: "safety" } matches if the array CONTAINS "safety". Separate dotted conditions may be met by different elements. $elemMatch: all conditions must hold in ONE element. $elemMatch in a filter picks documents; it does not trim the array.

